data.content:
**No Thread.Abort migration is needed here.**

Your code only uses `Thread.Start()`, `Thread.Join()`, and `Thread.Sleep()` — none of these are affected by the .NET 6+ breaking change. They work identically on .NET 8. There are no calls to `Thread.Abort`, no `ThreadAbortException` catch blocks, no `Thread.ResetAbort`, and no `Thread.Interrupt`. The code will compile and run correctly as-is on `net8.0`.

---

**Optional modernization** (unrelated to Thread.Abort migration):

You could simplify with `Parallel.ForEach` or `Task.WhenAll`:

```csharp
public class SimpleWorker
{
    public async Task RunBatchAsync(List<WorkItem> items, CancellationToken token = default)
    {
        await Parallel.ForEachAsync(items, token, async (item, ct) =>
        {
            ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
            await Task.Delay(100, ct); // replaces Thread.Sleep
            item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
        });
    }
}
```

This is purely an improvement for modern idioms (async, structured cancellation support) — not a required migration step.
